# The same proof with both public keys the identity, which algorithm
# 10.10 admits: it types them as "h in G_q" and "h' in G_q", again
# without the "\ {1}" it writes for the Schnorr base.
#
# The third equation -- the only one that says anything about the
# plaintexts -- collapses to 1 = 1^r * 1^(-r'), and our checker
# reports it as unsatisfiable whenever the two ciphertexts differ.
#
# It is worth being exact about what that does and does not catch.
# With both keys the identity an honest c1 and c1' are both equal to
# the message, so their quotient is the identity too, and on that
# input the checker reports the relation determined and acceptable --
# correctly, because the relation is fine.  It has simply stopped
# being a statement about plaintext equality.  No verdict of ours
# addresses that: whether a relation says what its author meant is
# not a question either axis decides.
secrets r r_
claims  r r_
eq  c0       g  1
eq  c0_      1  g
eq  c1/c1_   1  1

# An instance: one distinct generator per base name, so the
# environment is faithful and the verdict above transfers to it.
# The exponents are ours and carry no meaning; every element of a
# prime-order group is a generator power, so this is simply a way
# to write distinct elements down.
point g  2
